WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.820
How can a cell be ready to destroy itself within hours, and still never do it by

2
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:11.280
mistake? The killer is an enzyme called a caspase.

3
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.000
It cuts other proteins.

4
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:14.720
Nothing undoes a cut.

5
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.280
So where can the cell place its safeguard?

6
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:24.033
Every caspase is made ahead of time, as an inactive precursor, so it is ready to

7
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:29.593
act. An initiator, such as Caspace 9, starts as a single inactive chain.

8
00:00:30.093 --> 00:00:34.933
It turns on only when a platform gathers several copies, and they pair up.

9
00:00:35.413 --> 00:00:40.133
Then it cuts an executioner caspase, which is already paired but inactive.

10
00:00:40.653 --> 00:00:44.573
So the first step is a gathering, and every step after it is a cut.

11
00:00:45.383 --> 00:00:48.743
One part of the platform, Apaf -1, sits in the cytosol.

12
00:00:49.163 --> 00:00:53.403
The other part, cytochrome c, is kept behind the outer membrane of the

13
00:00:53.403 --> 00:00:55.843
mitochondrion, so no platform forms.

14
00:00:56.423 --> 00:00:59.723
If that membrane opens, cytochrome c escapes.

15
00:01:00.543 --> 00:01:06.963
Cytochrome c and the nucleotide dATP then make seven copies of Apaf -1 assemble into

16
00:01:06.963 --> 00:01:11.683
a wheel. This wheel, the apoptosome, gathers Caspase -9.

17
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:19.133
At that membrane, a family of proteins named for BCL-2 decides, and it has three

18
00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:24.013
classes. BACs and BAK, the effectors, open the outer membrane once they are

19
00:01:24.013 --> 00:01:28.813
activated. BCL-2 and its relatives, the guards, hold them in check.

20
00:01:29.353 --> 00:01:33.793
The sensors, such as BAD and BID, carry a short helix that fills a guard's groove.

21
00:01:34.353 --> 00:01:36.013
Some also switch on BACs.

22
00:01:37.683 --> 00:01:40.643
In a healthy cell, guards and effectors are both present.

23
00:01:41.043 --> 00:01:44.143
Their amounts and binding set how hard the cell must be pushed.

24
00:01:44.543 --> 00:01:47.703
A stress must switch on enough sensors to tie up the guards.

25
00:01:48.063 --> 00:01:52.063
Then the guards are occupied, backs and BAK are free, and the membrane opens.

26
00:01:52.883 --> 00:01:55.383
Cytochrome c escapes from the mitochondrion.

27
00:01:56.117 --> 00:01:58.037
Now follow the whole chain, in order.

28
00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:01.657
Enough sensors tie up the guards, and backs and BAK open the membrane.

29
00:02:02.317 --> 00:02:05.917
Cytochrome c escapes, and the wheel assembles and gathers cast -paste 9.

30
00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:08.457
Then the cuts begin, one after another.

31
00:02:09.017 --> 00:02:13.657
Keep this rule: the balance of guards and sensors mostly decides before the first

32
00:02:13.657 --> 00:02:15.757
cut, since no cut is undone.
